X:1
T:La Rana
T:The Frog
C:traditional (Mexico)
O:Mexico
R:cumulative song (children's song)
S:Eleanor Hague, Spanish-American Folk-Songs, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society 10 (1917), No. 76, pp. 98-99, as sung by Mrs. Dane Coolidge
N:A Mexican cumulative song and memory game: each verse brings a new creature or thing out to sing with the frog under the water, and the whole chain is sung back each time (Hague: "Each verse adds an animal, and the whole list is repeated"). The chain: la Araña (the spider), la Rana (the frog), el Ratón (the rat), el Gato (the cat), el Perro (the dog), el Palo (the stick), la Lumbre (the fire), el Agua (the water), el Buey (the ox), el Cochino (the pig), el Herrero (the blacksmith), la Muerte (Death), Dios (God); a class may stop at the blacksmith. End with the "Ga-ra" croaks, as many as you like, the last held as long as breath lasts. Hague's last bar of the verse has two quarters, the second under a fermata; it is written here as a quarter and a held half so the bar is full. The tempo is ours.
Q:1/4=112
M:3/4
L:1/8
K:D
