W:(The frog goes out for a walk, then comes the rat and sings: the rat and the frog, the spider and the frog, are singing under the water.)

X:2
T:Vámonos por Santa Anita
T:Come, Let's Go to Santa Anita
C:traditional (northern Mexico)
O:northern Mexico
R:play song
S:Eleanor Hague, Spanish-American Folk-Songs, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society 10 (1917), No. 75, p. 98, as sung by Mrs. Karbe
N:A light play song from northern Mexico: come, let's go to Santa Anita (a village outing), with the oxcarts in front and the oxen behind. Keep the dotted figure long-short, a skip, not even eighths; it suits skipping or a walking game in pairs. Sing the Spanish twice through (the repeat), then Hague's English singing translation. Hague marks it "Gayly"; the tempo is ours.
Q:1/4=100
M:2/4
L:1/16
K:G
